3.75% by a 9-3 vote, with three members dissenting in favor of a 25-basis-point hike, has become the dominant driver of trader positioning on any 2026 rate increase. June CPI easing to 3.5% year-over-year from 4.2% in May provided modest relief, yet the print remains well above the 2% target and follows earlier spikes linked to energy prices and Middle East tensions. Markets now price a meaningful probability of at least one hike before year-end, with the September 15-16 meeting and August 12 CPI release serving as near-term catalysts. Fed funds futures and the CME FedWatch Tool reflect this hawkish tilt, while the upcoming Jackson Hole symposium could clarify whether Chair Warsh and the majority view the current stance as sufficiently restrictive amid sticky core inflation.","context_requires_regen":false,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-05T22:16:21.988Z"},"version":"v1"}]
